[Chapter "Queen's Gambit Declined"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined"]
[ECO "D30"]
[Diagram "4...Be7"]
[Course "queens-gambit-declined"]
[WhiteLesson "Key ideas: Why the Queen's Gambit"]
[BlackLesson "Key ideas: Why the Queen's Gambit Declined"]

{Black holds d5 with the e-pawn: solid and classical, at the cost of shutting in the c8 bishop for now.

White: Develop with Nc3, Nf3 and Bg5, pinning the knight that guards d5. After an exchange on d5, push b4–b5 to create weaknesses on Black's queenside: the minority attack.

Black: Hold d5 with ...e6, ...Nf6 and ...Be7, then castle. Free your game with ...c5 or ...dxc4. Your problem piece is the c8 bishop, shut in behind e6: bring it out with ...b6 and ...Bb7, or after ...dxc4 and ...e5.}
1. d4 {Takes the center. The queen already guards d4.} d5 {Black claims an equal share of the center.}
2. c4 {The Queen's Gambit: White offers the c-pawn to lure Black's d-pawn away from the center.} e6 {This is the Queen's Gambit Declined. The e-pawn holds d5 but shuts in the c8 bishop.}
3. Nc3 {More pressure on d5.} Nf6 {Defends d5 again.}
4. Bg5 {Pins the knight that defends d5.} Be7 {Breaks the pin, ready to castle.} *

[Chapter "Semi-Slav Defense"]
[Opening "Semi-Slav Defense"]
[ECO "D43"]
[Diagram "4...c6"]
[Course "slav"]
[WhiteLesson "Semi-Slav: 4...e6"]

{Black adds ...c6 to the Declined setup, ready to take on c4 and hold the pawn with ...b5. Rich and often sharp.

White: Take on d5 with 5.cxd5. After ...exd5 the pawns are the same as in the Exchange Variation, with Black's pawn already on c6, so the plans are too: Bg5, e3, Bd3, Qc2, castle, and the minority attack.

Black: Build the c6–d5–e6 wall, then take on c4 once White's bishop has moved, so you gain a tempo. Expand with ...b5 and ...Bb7, then break with ...c5. Holding the c4-pawn after 5.Bg5 dxc4 leads to razor-sharp play.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 {Black declines the gambit.} 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 c6 {This is the Semi-Slav: the e6-pawn of the Queen's Gambit Declined plus the c6-pawn of the Slav. Black now threatens to take on c4 and hold the pawn with ...b5.} *

[Chapter "Tarrasch Defense"]
[Opening "Tarrasch Defense"]
[ECO "D32"]
[Diagram "4...exd5"]
[Course "tarrasch"]
[WhiteLesson "Rubinstein System: 4.cxd5 and 6.g3"]
[BlackLesson "Key ideas: Why the Tarrasch"]

{Black hits d4 with ...c5 at once, accepting an isolated d-pawn for free, active pieces.

White: Aim at the d5-pawn, which is often left isolated after ...cxd4. Blockade it on d4, press on it with Nf3, g3, Bg2 and your rooks, and trade minor pieces, because endgames favor the side playing against an isolated pawn.

Black: Accept an isolated d-pawn in return for free, active pieces: ...Nc6, ...Nf6, ...Be7 and castling. Keep pieces on and use the open c- and e-files. If White takes on c5 early, push ...d4! instead of taking back; later, push ...d4 when it opens the game for you.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 {More pressure on d5.} c5 {This is the Tarrasch Defense. Black hits d4 at once, ready to accept an isolated d-pawn for active pieces.}
4. cxd5 {White's most common reply.} exd5 {The d5-pawn often ends up isolated once the c-pawns are traded.} *

[Chapter "Queen's Gambit Accepted"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted"]
[ECO "D20"]
[Diagram "4...e5"]
[Course "queens-gambit-accepted"]
[WhiteLesson "3...Nf6"]
[BlackLesson "Key ideas: Why the Queen's Gambit Accepted"]

{Black takes the pawn to develop freely and lets White have the center for a while, then strikes back with ...e5 or ...c5.

White: Play 3.e3 and win the pawn back at once with Bxc4. Against ...e6 and ...c5, castle, then either trade queens on d8 for a safe endgame with slightly more active pieces, or keep the queens and push e4.

Black: Take on c4 to develop freely, then hit back at White's center at once with ...e5 or ...c5. Don't try to keep the pawn with an early ...b5: after 3.e3 b5 4.a4, Black's best is to give it back, and the natural 4...c6 can cost a piece. ...b5 works only when White's own pawns block the long diagonal, as in 3.Nc3 e5 4.d5 Nf6 5.e4 b5.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 {This is the Queen's Gambit Accepted. Black gives up the center for a moment.}
3. Nc3 {The most common move at club level. White prepares e4.} Nf6 {Develops and watches e4. 3...e5 at once is just as good, and it is what the Queen's Gambit Accepted Course plays as Black.}
4. e4 {White takes the whole center.} e5 {Black strikes back at once. 4...e6 is played more often, but it leaves White slightly better.} *

[Chapter "Slav Defense"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Two Knights Attack"]
[ECO "D15"]
[Diagram "4...dxc4"]
[Course "slav"]
[WhiteLesson "Czech Variation: 4...dxc4"]
[BlackLesson "Key ideas: Why the Slav"]

{Black guards d5 with the c-pawn, which keeps the c8 bishop's diagonal open. Solid, with a few exact lines to know.

White: Develop with Nc3 and Nf3. Against 4...dxc4, play 5.a4 to stop ...b5, then win the pawn back with e3 and Bxc4, castle, and prepare e4 with Qe2. If Black's bishop leaves c8 before Black takes on c4, cxd5 and Qb3 hit the b7-pawn it no longer guards.

Black: Take on c4 first, then bring the bishop out: after 5.a4, play 5...Bf5, ...e6, ...Bb4 and castle (the Czech Variation). If White doesn't play a4, keep the extra pawn with ...b5. Against an early e3, play ...Bf5 at once.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 {This is the Slav Defense. The c-pawn defends d5 and leaves the c8 bishop free.}
3. Nc3 {More popular than 3.Nf3 at club level.} Nf6 {Defends d5 and develops.}
4. Nf3 {The Three Knights Variation.} dxc4 {Black takes the pawn before bringing the bishop out. 4...Bf5?! at once lets White play cxd5 and Qb3, hitting b7.} *

[Chapter "Marshall Defense"]
[Group "Other variations"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Marshall Defense"]
[ECO "D06"]
[Diagram "4...Nf6"]
[Course "none"]

{Black develops the knight but doesn't hold d5 with a pawn. After 3.cxd5, White gains time chasing the piece that takes back. Stockfish rates it slightly better for White (+0.6).

White: Take on d5 at once. After 3...Nxd5, push 4.e4 to hit the knight and take the center, then develop with Nc3 and Nf3. After 3...Qxd5, 4.Nc3 hits the queen.

Black: Take back with the knight. After 4.e4 Nf6 5.Nc3, strike at the center at once with 5...e5 and trade queens on d4 (Stockfish +0.4). Slower moves let White keep a big center.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 Nf6 {This is the Marshall Defense. The knight develops, but no pawn supports d5.}
3. cxd5 {Takes on d5 at once, so Black has to take back with a piece.} Nxd5 {Three Black players in four take back with the knight.}
4. e4 {Hits the knight and takes the whole center.} Nf6 {Half of Black players retreat to f6, and almost as many to b6.} *

[Chapter "Albin Countergambit"]
[Group "Other variations"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Albin Countergambit"]
[ECO "D08"]
[Diagram "4...Nc6"]
[Course "none"]

{Black gives up the e-pawn so the d-pawn can cramp White from d4. A surprise weapon: Stockfish rates it slightly better for White (+0.7).

White: Keep the extra pawn and develop with Nf3, g3, Bg2, Nbd2 and castling. Don't play e3 early: 4.e3? Bb4+ 5.Bd2 dxe3 walks into the Lasker Trap. Later, round up the d4-pawn.

Black: Win back the e5-pawn with ...Nge7 and ...Ng6, after ...Nc6, while the d4-pawn cramps White. If White plays e3, check first with ...Bb4+, then take on e3.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e5 {This is the Albin Countergambit. Black offers the e-pawn to break up White's center.}
3. dxe5 d4 {The d-pawn advances and cramps White: it controls c3 and e3.}
4. Nf3 {Develops and guards e5.} Nc6 {Attacks e5 and supports d4.} *

[Chapter "Baltic Defense"]
[Group "Other variations"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Baltic Defense"]
[ECO "D06"]
[Diagram "4...Qxd5"]
[Course "none"]

{Black brings the c8 bishop out before ...e6 can shut it in. The cost is b7, which loses its guard. Stockfish rates it slightly better for White (+0.8).

White: Take on d5 with 3.cxd5. If Black takes back with 3...Qxd5?, 4.Nc3 hits the queen and wins a lot of time (Stockfish +2.8, winning for White). After 3...Bxb1 4.Rxb1 Qxd5, develop with Nf3 and e3 (Stockfish +0.9, slightly better for White).

Black: Against 3.cxd5, give up the bishop first: 3...Bxb1 4.Rxb1 Qxd5, then ...Nf6 and ...e6. Against 3.Nc3, play ...e6, ...Nf6 and ...Nc6. If White plays Qb3, answer with ...Nc6, not ...Nf6, which lets the queen take on b7 (Stockfish +1.0).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 Bf5 {[%engine ok] This is the Baltic Defense. The bishop comes out before ...e6 can shut it in.}
3. cxd5 {Stockfish's choice. 3.Nc3, six White players in ten, is a little slower.} Bxb1 {The best reply: the bishop takes the knight, so the queen can take back on d5 without being chased by Nc3. The common 3...Qxd5? runs into 4.Nc3.}
4. Rxb1 Qxd5 {Material is level, and White has the two bishops.} *

[Chapter "Austrian Defense"]
[Group "Other variations"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Austrian Defense"]
[ECO "D06"]
[Diagram "4...cxd4"]
[Course "none"]

{Black copies White's c-pawn move and hits d4 at once. The center opens early, and Black's queen comes out early too, so White gains time chasing it.

White: Take on d5 with 3.cxd5. After 3...Qxd5 4.Nf3 cxd4, 5.Nc3 gains time on the queen, and Nxd4 wins the pawn back (Stockfish +0.5, slightly better for White).

Black: Take back with the queen and trade on d4. After 5.Nc3, step the queen out to a5 and develop with ...Nf6.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c5 {This is the Austrian Defense. Black hits d4 at once.}
3. cxd5 {Stockfish's choice. 3.dxc5 is just as common.} Qxd5 {Three Black players in four take back with the queen.}
4. Nf3 {Develops and guards d4.} cxd4 {Takes the pawn. 5.Nc3 hits the queen, and White takes back on d4 next.} *

[Chapter "Chigorin Defense"]
[Group "Other variations"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Chigorin Defense"]
[ECO "D07"]
[Diagram "4...Bxf3"]
[Course "none"]

{Black develops the knight in front of the c-pawn and plays for quick piece pressure on d4, often giving up a bishop for a knight.

White: Develop with Nf3. After 3...Bg4, take on d5 at once: 4.cxd5 Bxf3 5.gxf3 Qxd5 6.e3 gives White the two bishops and the center (Stockfish +0.9, slightly better for White).

Black: Pin the f3 knight with ...Bg4, and press on d4 with the knight and queen. After 4.cxd5, take on f3 first, then take back on d5 with the queen.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 Nc6 {This is the Chigorin Defense. The knight blocks the c-pawn but hits d4.}
3. Nf3 {Develops and guards d4.} Bg4 {Pins the knight that guards d4. This is the main line.}
4. cxd5 {Takes on d5 before Black can add more pressure.} Bxf3 {Stockfish's choice. 4...Qxd5, played more often, lets White gain time with 5.Nc3 (Stockfish +1.6, clearly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Catalan Opening"]
[Group "Other variations"]
[Opening "Catalan Opening: Closed"]
[ECO "E06"]
[Diagram "5.Bg2"]
[Course "queens-gambit-declined"]
[BlackLesson "Catalan: 4.g3"]

{White puts the f1 bishop on g2, where it presses on d5 and on Black's queenside. White often lets Black take on c4 and wins the pawn back later.

White: Play Bg2 and castle. If Black takes on c4, win the pawn back with Qc2 or Qa4, and use the bishop on g2 against Black's queenside.

Black: Develop with ...Be7 and castle, then take on c4 once White has castled. Blunt the g2 bishop with ...a6, ...b5 and ...Bb7, or with ...Bd7 and ...Bc6.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. g3 {This is the Catalan Opening. The bishop will go to g2.} Be7 {The most common reply. Black develops and castles before taking on c4.}
5. Bg2 {The bishop presses on d5 and on the long diagonal.} *

[Chapter "Ragozin Defense"]
[Group "Other variations"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Ragozin Defense"]
[ECO "D38"]
[Diagram "4...Bb4"]
[Course "none"]

{Black pins the c3 knight, as in the Nimzo-Indian, so White can't easily push e4.

White: Pin back with 5.Bg5, the most common move and Stockfish's choice. After 5...h6 6.Bxf6 Qxf6 7.e3, the game is quiet and equal (Stockfish +0.1).

Black: Answer 5.Bg5 with ...h6, castle and take on c4, then hit the center with ...c5.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 Bb4 {This is the Ragozin Defense. The bishop pins the knight on c3.} *

[Chapter "Semi-Slav: Moscow Variation"]
[Group "Other variations"]
[Opening "Semi-Slav Defense: Moscow Variation"]
[ECO "D43"]
[Diagram "6...Qxf6"]
[Course "none"]

{After 5.Bg5 h6, White gives up the bishop for the knight. Black gets the two bishops, and White gets quicker development.

White: Play 7.e3, then Be2, castle and Qc2 (Stockfish +0.2, equal).

Black: Develop with ...Nd7 and ...Be7, castle, and use the two bishops once the position opens.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 c6 {The Semi-Slav.}
5. Bg5 {Pins the knight.} h6 {Asks the bishop to decide.}
6. Bxf6 {This is the Moscow Variation. 6.Bh4, three times as common, allows the sharp Anti-Moscow Gambit: 6...dxc4 7.e4 g5 8.Bg3 b5.} Qxf6 {Black has the two bishops.} *
